Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) is an essential tool for studying fluid flow and has been widely applied in various fields, including engineering, aerospace, automotive design, and climate research. The data generated from CFD simulations typically contain complex spatiotemporal information, and the temporal prediction of this data holds significant practical importance in many applications. CFD solves the governing equations of fluid mechanics through numerical methods, providing detailed information about the flow field, including physical quantities such as velocity, pressure, and temperature. However, these data are often complex and exhibit high spatiotemporal dynamics, particularly in the prediction of turbulence, which makes temporal prediction a challenging task.
This study aims to employ Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to predict the velocity data of flow around a cylinder from CFD simulations, with the goal of improving the accuracy and reliability of predictions. By simplifying the model structure, Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and normalization techniques were used to effectively reduce the data dimensionality, enhancing both training efficiency and stability. During model training, appropriate parameter combinations were selected to enable the LSTM to effectively capture the dynamic features within the flow data. Experimental results, measured by the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), show that the model performs well in predicting the velocity of flow around a cylinder. Compared with traditional prediction methods, the LSTM model demonstrates better performance in handling complex flow patterns, especially in capturing nonlinear characteristics and dynamic changes. Furthermore, when compared with the full LSTM model, the simplified version not only maintains comparable or slightly improved accuracy but also significantly reduces training time.
This report proposes the use of a simplified LSTM for temporal prediction of CFD velocity data in cylindrical flow, aiming to explore its effectiveness in capturing the dynamic features of the flow and its potential applications in fluid dynamics. Particularly in turbulence prediction, this approach offers a new perspective for fluid dynamics research by saving computational resources and improving prediction efficiency.
Keywords: Computational Fluid Dynamics, Long Short-Term Memory, Temporal Prediction, Data-Driven, Turbulence Prediction
